{
  "id": "refund_reversal_chain",
  "title": "A refund_reversal lowers refunded and nets against its adjustment",
  "test_plan_vector": null,
  "spec": [
    "Platform Verification 8: SHOULD reconcile refunds against Order adjustments. For each `event.ref.adjustment_id` cited, the platform sums `event.amount` over the `succeeded` `refund` receipts that cite it, minus `succeeded` `refund_reversal` receipts that cite it, counting each movement once per Running Totals, and compares the result with the magnitude of that adjustment's `total` entry. `pending` and `failed` receipts are not counted. A cited `adjustment_id` that matches no adjustment is also `reconciliation_adjustment_mismatch`. The outcome is reported on the last `succeeded` receipt that cites the adjustment. A mismatch is a commercial problem, not a cryptographic one: the receipts remain valid.",
    "Running Totals: `totals.mandated` is the `payment_amount` of the Payment Mandate that `mandate_ref` covers. The other totals start at 0 and change only on `succeeded` events: `authorization` and `authorization_adjustment` add to `authorized`, `release` subtracts from it, `capture` adds to `captured`, `capture_reversal` subtracts from it, `refund` adds to `refunded`, `refund_reversal` subtracts from it. A capture does not reduce `authorized`.",
    "Edge Cases, Refund returned after success: a refund that was receipted as `succeeded` and later fails or is returned by the issuer gets a `refund_reversal` receipt with the same `event.ref`, which lowers `refunded`."
  ],
  "signing": "TEST-ONLY P-256 keys derived from fixed labels in common/payments/ap2_receipt_fixtures.py; deterministic ECDSA (RFC 6979). Never use them outside these vectors.",
  "notes": "refunded goes 5400, 0, 5400. The reversal has the same type-distinct event.ref as the refund it reverses, so it is not a follow-up. Reconciliation: 5400 - 5400 + 5400 matches the 5400 adjustment.",
